description:
In forward.py:_merge_sequential_branches (lines 816-883), nested if/else branches in forward() are treated as sequential branches and flattened to the top level. The inner branch's _BranchNode sentinel appears after the outer branch in the flat node list, instead of being inside the outer branch's true arm.

Repro:
  class Nested(ForwardConstruct):
      a = Node.scripted('a', ...)
      b = Node.scripted('b', ...)
      c = Node.scripted('c', ...)
      d = Node.scripted('d', ...)
      e = Node.scripted('e', ...)

      def forward(self, x):
          r = self.a(x)
          if r.score > 0.5:          # branch 0
              r2 = self.b(r)
              if r2.confidence > 0.8:  # branch 1 (nested)
                  return self.c(r2)
              else:
                  return self.d(r2)
          else:
              return self.e(r)

The compiled graph would have branch 1 always evaluated regardless of branch 0's outcome, because both _BranchNode sentinels are at the same level in the node list.

The re-trace strategy only flips one branch at a time (line 719: branch_decisions={branch.branch_id: False}). For nested branches, branch 1 only exists in the outer True arm. When branch 0 is flipped to False, branch 1 is never encountered, so the trace correctly shows the False arm. But _merge_sequential_branches treats the two traces as independent sequential branches instead of recognizing the nesting.

Severity: P3 because the docstring already documents this as 'sequential branches (not nested)' — it's a known limitation, not a silent bug. But there's no error/warning when a user writes nested branches, leading to silently wrong graph topology.

Fix options:
1. Detect nesting (branch N only appears when branch M is True) and build a tree of _BranchMeta instead of a flat list
2. At minimum, raise ConstructError when nested branches are detected, with a hint to use sub-constructs

